gular,strong,without-article"],["geheimen","geheimer","genitive"]]},"gouverneurinnen":{"entries":[["gouverneurinnen",null,null,null]],"senses":[[710365,"gouverneurinnen","noun","plural of Gouverneurin",0,"f"]],"inflections":[["gouverneurinnen","gouverneurin","plural"]]},"hüttenwerke":{"entries":[["hüttenwerke",null,null,null]],"senses":[[232602,"hüttenwerke","noun","nominative/accusative/genitive plural of Hüttenwerk",0,"n"]],"inflections":[["hüttenwerke","hüttenwerk","plural"]]},"katalogisierst":{"entries":[["katalogisierst",null,null,null]],"senses":[[127988,"katalogisierst","verb","second-person singular present of katalogisieren",0]],"inflections":[["katalogisierst","katalogisieren","indicative,present,second-person,singular"]]},"kegel":{"entries":[["kegel",null,"/ˈkeːɡəl/","From Middle High German kegel, from Old High German chegil, kegil (“peg”), especially pegs for a tent (zeltchegil) or similar. Middle High German kegel means “peg or pin used for a game” (such as skittles), but regional use from the 14th century onward also preserve a more general meaning of “peg, cudgel”.\nThe etymology cannot be traced with certainty. The word is a member of a Wortsippe which also includes Keil (“wedge”), German Kugel (“ball, sphere”) and Kiel (“keel”).\nNo cognates of Kegel are known outside of German (other than loans from German in Dutch, Scandinavian as well as Baltic and Slavic languages). French quille seems to be a reflex of an Old Frankish form of the word. Proto-Slavic *žezlъ (“stick, staff, rod”) has diverse proposed origins.\nThe German word Keil (“wedge”) is closely related, compared by Grimm to the relationship of Latin cuneus (“wedge”) with cōnus (“cone”). In some dialects, Keil was used as the term for both “wedge” and “bowling pin”. On the other hand, Kroonen suggests a derivation from Proto-Germanic *kagilaz (“cone, pin”), from the root underlying Proto-Germanic *kagô (“trunk”) + *-ilaz (“diminutive suffix, agent suffix”).\nAlso closely related is Kugel (“ball, sphere”). All these terms developed in context of the game of skittles. The relation of Keil to Kegel (both terms for the pin) was the same as that of Kaule to Kugel (both terms for the ball; the former remained dialectal, while Kugel became the generic term for \"sphere\").\nFor the development of the sense of an illegitimate son compare German Bengel, Flegel, Balg and the like.",{"g":"m","decl":"|||s|||n|"}]],"senses":[[18696,"kegel","noun","skittle, ninepin, bowling pin; pin",0,"m"],[18697,"kegel","noun","any heap or peak of conical or pyramidal shape, especially of feces, hay, brushwood, of mountains and of the tip of a helmet",1,"m"],[18698,"kegel","noun","cone",2,"m"],[18699,"kegel","noun","ruffian, bastard (illegitimate child)",3,"m",["obsolete"]]],"related_forms":[[28438,"kegel","kegelförmig","derived"],[28439,"kegel","kegelbahn","related"],[28440,"kegel","kegelbruder","related"],[28441,"kegel","kegeldach","related"],[28442,"kegel","kegelkopfschrecke","related"],[28443,"kegel","kegelstumpf","related"],[28444,"kegel","mit kind und kegel","related"]],"inflections":[["kegel","kegeln","first-person,indicative,present,singular"]]},"krepiertest":{"entries":[["krepiertest",null,null,null]],"senses":[[128499,"krepiertest","verb","inflection of krepieren:",0],[128500,"krepiertest","verb","second-person singular preterite",1],[128501,"krepiertest","verb","second-person singular subjunctive II",2]],"inflections":[["krepiertest","krepieren","indicative,preterite,second-person,singular"]]},"leid":{"entries":[["leid",null,"/laɪ̯t/","From Middle High German leit from Old High German leid, from Proto-West Germanic *laiþ, whence also English loath and Old Norse leiðr; also Latin laedō (“strike, betray”).",{"g":"n","decl":"||/e|es/s|-|-|-|-"}]],"senses":[[21058,"leid","adj","distressing, uncomfortable",0],[21059,"leid","noun","woe, grief, distress, sorrow, suffering, affliction",1,"n"],[21060,"leid","noun","wrong, harm, injury",2,"n"]],"inflections":[["leid","leiden","i